@@ -0,0 +1,232 @@
+# Private offline WAHA A/B checkpoint
+
+The minimal fixture compiles the real `src/core/engines/gows/ab.adapter.ts` and
+its local generated protobuf client. The eight `WhatsappSessionGoWSCore`
+overrides call that same adapter. It never starts NestJS, WAHA sessions, an
+account, a database, QR flow, or the WhatsApp SDK runtime. The HTTP fixture
+imports the actual API context wrapper and NestJS exception classes. Only the
+synthetic Go gRPC fixture and optional HTTP router listen, on `127.0.0.1` and
+random ports. The minimal HTTP router is test infrastructure. An additional
+opt-in harness below separately tests the actual compiled NestJS controllers.
+
+## Reproduce
+
+Node 24.19.0 and npm 11.9.0 were available in the selected workspace. From this
+repository root:
+
+```sh
+npm ci --prefix checkpoints/offline-ab
+npm run generate --prefix checkpoints/offline-ab
+node scripts/gows-proto.js check
+npm test --prefix checkpoints/offline-ab
+```
+
+The locked minimal dependencies include TypeScript 5.9.2, grpc-tools 1.13.0,
+protoc-gen-ts 0.8.7, grpc-js 1.14.1, and google-protobuf 3.21.4. Generated
+bindings come only from the checked-in `src/core/engines/gows/proto/gows.proto`,
+copied from the companion GOWS checkpoint. The manifest records SHA-256 hashes
+of that source and the three generated outputs. Generation fails on tool errors.
+Release downloading is removed from the generation workflow.
+
+Start the companion Go `ab-offline-fixture` binary. It prints a loopback
+address. Pass that address to the Node fixture:
+
+```sh
+node checkpoints/offline-ab/vertical.cjs 127.0.0.1:PORT
+node checkpoints/offline-ab/http-bridge.cjs --grpc 127.0.0.1:PORT --port-file /tmp/ab-http-ready.json
+```
+
+The bridge prints
+`{ "address": "http://127.0.0.1:PORT", "fixtureRouter": true }`. It implements
+the existing eight HTTP routes for the synthetic vertical test;
+`GET /__fixture/stats` exposes the number of RPC invocations and
+`POST /__fixture/reset` resets that fixture counter. SIGTERM closes the fixture
+HTTP server and client. The new read-only product probe is
+`GET /api/contacts/ab-capabilities?session=...`, with the existing session read
+policy. It reads the provider's static `gows-ab-v1` contract and exact operation
+inventory without Activity, a database, or a WhatsApp operation. Existing MCP
+tools are retained; the probe has no separate MCP tool.
+
+## Evidence and limits
+
+The twenty-two Node tests use generated protobuf serialization/deserialization
+and cover all eight operations, both star values, all three pin durations, unpin
+zero duration, session/client lazy resolution, PN/LID/group keys, About value /
+empty / null / error, invalid session / JID / key / duration, and uncertain /
+transport / missing / mismatched-intent responses without application retry. The
+loopback vertical runner additionally exercises the actual generated Node client
+against the Go service and fake backend. Provider `accepted` means accepted for
+submission; it is not proof of phone-side observation.
+
+Durable `X-WhatsApp-Intent-Id` and `X-WhatsApp-Intent-Timestamp-Ms` headers pass
+through an AsyncLocalStorage context into all seven mutations unchanged. Tests
+cover separate concurrent contexts, frozen timestamps, malformed/partial headers
+before any hook or RPC, and the static capability probe without session lookup.
+Both headers may be absent for a direct REST request; only then does the adapter
+generate a new local intent. The API wrapper returns an uncertain mutation as
+HTTP 502 with `outcome: uncertain` and its intent id. Typed predispatch provider
+rejections retain HTTP 400/404/412 (and authentication 403/401) with
+`outcome: failed_before_send`; read input validation returns 400. The actual
+`src/api/ab.context.ts` module is also strictly compiled and tested with real
+NestJS exception classes; this does not start a Nest application.
+
+`GET /api/:session/chats/:chatId/archive-state` reads the local provider cache.
+It preserves `known`, `archived`, and `source` and always returns
+`phoneFresh: false`. Its local PN normalization avoids identifier hooks that
+could trigger cold-number lookup. Cache reads and the static contract probe have
+no Activity decorator.
+
+Targets and historical key fields use local canonicalization only: `@c.us`
+becomes `@s.whatsapp.net`; PN, LID, and group namespaces and their user parts
+remain unchanged. Bare numeric targets are supported, while serialized keys
+require explicit namespaces. Device identifiers, unsupported namespaces, and
+incomplete group participants are rejected before any RPC. Mutable phone or
+alias hooks are not used for A/B operations. Tests load the real legacy JID
+normalizer to demonstrate the rejected rewrites, verify PN/LID preservation, and
+preserve unrelated engines' internal TypeErrors.
+
+`make proto-gows` builds the checked-in source. To adopt a reviewed companion
+contract change, explicitly copy `../gows/proto/gows.proto` over the local
+source before generating and reviewing the diff. Unsupported CLI arguments such
+as the old `--dir` flag fail instead of silently selecting a different source.
+
+The production overrides retain `@Activity()`. This minimal fixture
+intentionally does not execute presence hooks. Full NestJS compilation and full
+locked WAHA dependency installation are separate build attempts reported in the
+checkpoint build evidence. Passing this adapter build does not establish full
+WAHA build, boot, authentication, routing, WhatsApp behavior, real-world pin
+duration, or account-state migration compatibility.
+
+## Historical message identity guard
+
+`message-identity.test.cjs` exercises the compiled identity guard and extracts
+the actual `getChatMessage` and `editMessage` method bodies from the session
+source into an inert fixture. The generated protobuf request classes and the
+actual `parseJson` function are used. Session construction, Activity, media
+processing, mutable alias hooks, the SDK and network calls are not executed.
+
+The legacy GOWS lookup still accepts only a short message ID. WAHA now compares
+its raw result to the requested chat and message ID before returning content or
+processing media. Serialized keys also bind the direction and supplied sender.
+An edit additionally requires a stored own message, verified before dispatch;
+an absent or inaccessible source never falls back to an unverified edit.
+Identity mismatches return a generic HTTP 422 without exposing stored content
+or identifiers. Provider failures propagate without a retry.
+
+Legacy short IDs and three-part serialized group keys remain accepted with
+their chat scope; a four-part key additionally checks its participant.
+Canonicalization is local: customer PN suffixes and device suffixes map to
+their GOWS non-device form, while PN and LID remain distinct. No phone lookup
+or alias hook changes a historical key. The stricter A/B full-key contract is
+unchanged. These guards complement the companion GOWS storage collision and
+edit checks; the short-ID RPC and storage schema have not been migrated.
+
+Ten additional offline tests cover cross-chat short-ID collisions, forged
+directions and participants, missing/malformed provider data, malformed
+serialized keys, legacy inputs, edit dispatch ordering and lookup/mutation
+errors. Their passing result does not certify live cache freshness, PN/LID
+mapping, linked-device convergence or WhatsApp acceptance.
+
+## Offline media conversion and handoff
+
+GOWS downloads preserve original Lottie sticker bytes (`application/was` ZIP),
+including their typed source SHA256 and size. No transparent WebP conversion
+runs in this engine. An absent source filename becomes `MESSAGE_ID.zip`.
+Other engines are unchanged, and old WebP cache entries are neither overwritten
+nor deleted. The original ZIP has a separate cache key. Run
+`npm run test:lottie --prefix checkpoints/offline-ab` after the full locked
+dependency installation to exercise the actual GOWS processor and MediaManager
+with a valid synthetic ZIP, inert download RPC and in-memory storage. This
+qualifies original-byte retrieval, not animated preview or live download.
+
+With local `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` installed, run:
+
+```sh
+npm run test:media --prefix checkpoints/offline-ab
+```
+
+The six opt-in tests invoke the actual `fileToMedia`, `sendMedia`, `sendVoice`
+and `sendVideo` method bodies, the real converter and temporary-file helper,
+and generated protobuf classes. The cleanup delay is shortened in the fixture.
+RPC, identifier hooks and SDK waveform/duration calculation are replaced by
+inert fixtures. No HTTP fetch, account connection or WhatsApp send occurs.
+
+Audio with `convert: false` keeps its exact bytes, MIME and filename. With
+`convert: true`, real ffmpeg produces Ogg/Opus mono at 48 kHz; ffprobe checks
+the container/codec and ffmpeg decodes the result. After successful conversion,
+the output filename uses a sanitized basename and `.ogg` for audio or `.mp4`
+for video/PTV. An already correct extension is preserved; missing names receive
+`audio.ogg` or `video.mp4`. Names are unchanged when conversion is disabled.
+Conversion failure prevents the send RPC, without retry or original-content
+fallback. Video/PTV tests use a synthetic converter and qualify metadata and
+handoff only, not video encoding. These results do not certify playback or
+acceptance by WhatsApp clients.
+
+About is `string | null`, preserving empty strings without attributing absence
+to privacy. Archive accepts an empty supplied message range; the Go provider
+uses available local state without fetching history. Star uses a complete key;
+message pin uses the typed pin RPC and requires a group participant. Uncertain
+mutations throw with the stable intent id. The shared MessageService client has
+gRPC retries disabled.
+
+Plugin integration and its policies for personal star versus visible message pin
+are covered in the companion checkpoint. Provider edition boundaries and
+packaging/distribution qualification remain outside this offline fixture.
+
+The integration stack can expose its fixture HTTP router through a Unix socket
+shared with the host while all Node/Go processes stay in the container with
+network disabled:
+
+```sh
+docker exec --user 1000:1000 checkpoint-ab-tools node /checkpoint/repositories/waha/checkpoints/offline-ab/fixture-stack.cjs --fixture /out/ab-offline-fixture --socket-file /out/offline-http.fixture.sock --ready-file /out/offline-stack.ready.json
+```
+
+`fixture-stack.cjs` first runs the eight-operation vertical test, then holds the
+Go fake backend and Node fixture HTTP router open. SIGTERM stops both. The Unix
+socket path must be new; the router refuses to overwrite an existing path.
+
+For positive message-source preflight, the HTTP fixture serves a synthetic
+`SOURCE` message for `15550001111@c.us` in session `fixture-a`, through the
+standard `/api/fixture-a/chats/:chatId/messages/:messageId?downloadMedia=false`
+route and the fixture convenience `/api/fixture-a/messages/:messageId` route. It
+returns explicit false lifetime flags and synthetic content; it performs no
+provider SDK request. Every A/B operation still crosses the real adapter and
+gRPC handlers to the Go fake backend.
+
+The opt-in `nest-controller.test.cjs` uses the full compiled WAHA build and its
+locked dependencies from `/tmp/waha-full` in `checkpoint-ab-tools`. It loads the
+actual `dist/api/{contacts,chats,chatting}.controller.js`, DTOs, decorators, API
+context wrapper, adapter and protobuf client. The real controller-level
+ValidationPipe and WorkingSessionPipe run. No global ValidationPipe is added,
+matching `src/main.ts`. The real controllers are not copied or reimplemented.
+
+The harness replaces only the manager/session DI/type module tokens with inert
+classes and injects a fake SessionManager returning the real adapter facade. The
+actual manager/session modules, application bootstrap, SDKs, databases and
+subprocess bootstrap stay unloaded; an import guard enforces that boundary.
+PoliciesGuard is explicitly overridden for this routing test. Authentication,
+provider authorization, presence Activity, full application startup, account
+lifecycle and WhatsApp behavior are outside this test's claim.
+
+The matching full build was compiled with the companion
+`build/run-waha-full-build.sh` script after `yarn install --immutable`; its
+underlying build command is:
+
+```sh
+docker exec --workdir /tmp/waha-full checkpoint-ab-tools node /tmp/waha-full/node_modules/@nestjs/cli/bin/nest.js build
+```
+
+With the Go fake backend already running, the executed qualification command is
+below. For a new fixture process, replace the gRPC address with the `grpc` field
+in `/out/offline-stack.ready.json`.
+
+```sh
+docker exec --user 1000:1000 --env AB_NEST_RUNTIME_ROOT=/tmp/waha-full --env AB_NEST_GRPC_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:32811 checkpoint-ab-tools node --test /checkpoint/repositories/waha/checkpoints/offline-ab/nest-controller.test.cjs
+```
+
+The isolated Nest test application listens on a temporary Unix socket and closes
+at test teardown. Its eleven Node tests cover all eight operations, fifteen real
+gRPC calls, exact verbs/paths and rejected wrong verbs, headers and concurrent
+intent isolation, capability/cache reads, real duration validation, rejection
+before RPC, and uncertain HTTP 502 without retry. The log and JSON result record
+exit status, source/compiled module hashes and the container network mode.
